sections.body:
At closing Seller will sign:

- a **third-party authorization** letting the lender and servicer talk with Buyer and the servicing company about the Existing Loans; and
- a separate **limited power of attorney**, signed with the formalities the law of {{state}} requires (usually notarization), authorizing Buyer only to:
  - request loan balances, payoff statements, payment histories and escrow analyses;
  - make payments and set up online payment access, while Seller keeps the right to view the account;
  - communicate with the hazard and flood insurers about the Property;
  - deal with utilities, the owners' association and taxing authorities about the Property; and
  - sign documents needed to pay off or refinance the Existing Loans in full, with the payoff going to the lender.

**The power of attorney will not allow Buyer to borrow money, open credit, modify or extend the Existing Loans, or sign any other document in Seller's name.** It ends when the Existing Loans are paid off. Seller may revoke it for Buyer's uncured default. Seller: [[initial:seller]]
